  4. Detailed Findings and Remediation
4.1 Hardcoded password (High)
  Risk: Anyone with access to the source code or repository can read the secret.
  Before:
  PASSWORD = "admin123"
  After:
  import os
  PASSWORD = os.environ["APP_PASSWORD"]
4.2 SQL injection (High)
  Risk: An input like ' OR '1'='1 changes the query and can expose or modify the whole database.
  Before:
    q = "SELECT * FROM users WHERE name = '" + name + "'"
    return db.execute(q).fetchall()
  After:
    return db.execute("SELECT * FROM users WHERE name = ?", (name,)).fetchall()
4.3 Command injection (High)
  Risk: Input such as 8.8.8.8; del important.txt runs extra commands on the system.
  Before:
    return subprocess.run("ping " + host, shell=True)
  After:
    import ipaddress
    ipaddress.ip_address(host)  # raises an error if not a valid IP
    return subprocess.run(["ping", host])
4.4 Weak password hashing (Medium)
  Risk: MD5 is fast and broken, so stolen hashes can be cracked quickly.
  Before:
    return hashlib.md5(pw.encode()).hexdigest()
  After:
    import bcrypt  # pip install bcrypt
    return bcrypt.hashpw(pw.encode(), bcrypt.gensalt())
4.5 Use of eval() (High)
  Risk: eval executes any Python code the user supplies.
  Before:
    return eval(code)
  After:
    import ast
    return ast.literal_eval(code)  # only accepts simple literals
    If evaluation is not truly needed, remove the function.
5. Secure Coding Best Practices
•	Never trust user input: validate and sanitize it.
•	Use parameterized queries for all database access.
•	Keep secrets in environment variables or a secrets manager, never in code.
•	Avoid eval, exec and shell=True.
•	Hash passwords with bcrypt or argon2, with a salt.
•	Apply least privilege and keep dependencies updated.
•	Run static analysis (such as Bandit) regularly, and review code manually as well.
6. Conclusion
The review found 5 vulnerabilities, 4 High and 1 Medium. All can be fixed with small code changes, shown above. Combining an automated tool with manual review caught each issue and showed how user input becomes dangerous when it is not handled safely.
